{"context": "A research team was considering whether to fund work on automating conceptual alignment research, such as scaffolding, datasets, and RL environments for fuzzy safety research tasks. They noted current models perform poorly at generating high-quality conceptual ideas and seem not to try hard, that good fuzzy RL tasks are hard to construct because grading is bottlenecked by conceptual understanding, and that many grants in this area would likely fail early.\n\nQuestion: Should the team pursue automated conceptual alignment research despite its uncertainty and difficulty?", "options": ["Fund automated conceptual alignment research despite its uncertainty, while shutting down any projects that would differentially accelerate capabilities.", "Pursue it only after demonstrating on a mundane grading task that the model knows the right answer elsewhere, so funding does not reward capability failures dressed as depth."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
